Title: Unlocking a Locked User Account in On-Premises Active Directory

Applies To: IT Support / Helpdesk Teams
Product/Service: Microsoft Active Directory (On-Premises)
Last Updated: 07/09/2025

Issue

A user is unable to log in and reports that their account is locked. The issue is typically caused by multiple failed password attempts or an application/device repeatedly trying to sign in with an outdated password.

Resolution Steps:

Step 1: Verify the Account Lockout

Log in to the Domain Controller or a server with the Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC) console installed.

Open Active Directory Users and Computers (ADUC):

Press Windows Key + R, type dsa.msc, and press Enter.

Navigate to the user’s Organizational Unit (OU).

Right-click the user account and select Properties.

Check the Account tab for the status. If locked, you’ll see the “Account is locked out” box checked.

Step 2: Unlock the Account

In the Account tab, uncheck the Account is locked out box.

Click Apply, then OK.

Inform the user their account is unlocked and ask them to try logging in again.

Step 4: Identify Cause (Optional but Recommended)

Check if the user’s account is being locked due to:

Multiple login attempts from different devices.

Mobile devices or Outlook using an old password.

Scheduled tasks or mapped drives with cached credentials.

Update or remove stored passwords from these sources.

Additional Notes

If the account repeatedly locks, review Event Viewer on the Domain Controller for lockout events (Event ID 4740).

Escalate to the System Administrator if the issue persists across multiple unlocks.

